WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.230
I only have three main points in my presentation

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.070
Other than that

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.250
I don’t have anything else to say

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.260
Those main points can be elaborated on

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.230 
Sometimes you need to explain further

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.100
so the audience can understand the content better

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.190
Elaboration means you provide more details

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.090
You could say “let me give you some more information” 

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:032.060
or “let me elaborate on …”

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:35.270 
as an introduction to further explain your points

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:41.050
And, use facts or statistics to support your statements

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.020
Say something like “based on our findings …,”

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.170
“according to a recent study …,”

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:49.260 
or “the data shows …”

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.180
What if the audience misunderstands me

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.180
or gets confused?

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:59.170 
Should I wait until the very end of the presentation 

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.070
to answer their questions? 

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.200
Whenever you feel a point needs to be clarified

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.190
use “what I mean is…” or “in other words…”

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.170 
to rephrase and clarify your meaning

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:16.170
You could also say “to put it simply”

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:20.190 
or "let me just say that another way."

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.280 
The better the elaboration

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.200
the less confused the audience will be

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.100
Good idea

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.110
What if the audience has a different opinion

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:34.020
after my presentation? 

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.010
How can I persuade them?

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.270
When you present an argument

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.140 
you should state why the audience

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.080
should support your idea

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.170
Giving some examples might convince them

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:50.070
and help them understand the concept better

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.150
You could use phrases like

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.130 
“for instance…,” “to illustrate…”

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.140 
or “let’s take this example…”

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.180
You can also use analogies

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:06.180 
to compare an abstract idea with a concrete one

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.220
An analogy is a comparison between two things

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.180 
that have similar features

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.020
Here is a well-known example of an analogy

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.120 
“Just like a sword is the weapon of a warrior

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.260 
a pen is the weapon of a writer.”

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:27.080
That’s a really good analogy. 

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:31.270
Make your analogy easy for the audience to relate to

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:35.230 
It needs to be short and simple

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.280
Couldn’t agree with you more

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.190
We are learning something new and true every day

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.070 
Like the analogy says:

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.110 
The truth in every myth is the pearl in every oyster

